// 030 — Unsigned vs signed integers — DESIGN NOTE
// C exposes `int` vs `unsigned int`, `int64_t` vs `uint64_t`. The
// range doubles on the positive side at the cost of one very sharp
// bug class: `unsigned a - b` where a < b wraps to a huge positive
// number, `for (unsigned i = n; i >= 0; i--)` never terminates,
// `size_t` in a comparison against a signed int silently promotes
// the signed side and compares wrong.
//
// AiLang has one Integer type: 64-bit signed. Range is ±9.22e18 —
// large enough that "I need the extra bit of headroom" is rare, and
// when it isn't, the answer is a bigint (see 031), not doubling the
// failure modes of signed/unsigned mixing.
//
// Bit-level operations (And, Or, Xor, Shl, Shr) are available for
// the 0..2^64 range when you're explicitly treating an Integer as a
// bitstring — that's a different concern from "what does arithmetic
// mean on this value," and it doesn't need a separate type.
SubRoutine.Main {
    max_i64 = 9223372036854775807
    min_i64 = Subtract(0, 9223372036854775807)
    min_i64 = Subtract(min_i64, 1)

    PrintMessage("i64 max = ")
    PrintNumber(max_i64)
    PrintMessage("\ni64 min = ")
    PrintNumber(min_i64)
    PrintMessage("\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
